// There are two kinds of generator and **the entry point a module exports is which kind it is**
// (docs/design/meta.md §13, decided 2026-10-29): `generate(d: Decl)` is called once per
// `#[derive(X)]`, and `generate_module(t: Target)`/`generate_query(t, f)` once per
// `#![repository(..)]`-style module attribute.
//
// The kind is read off the entry point rather than named in the attribute because the entry point
// has to exist either way, so an attribute that also named the kind would be a second source of
// truth that could disagree with it. The cost of that choice is this refusal: a module exporting
// both says it is both, and nothing would say which one a target meant.
#![generator(Csv)]

pub fn generate(x: int) -> int {
    return x
}

pub fn generate_module(x: int) -> int {
    return x
}

fn main() {
    print(generate(1))
}
